  "question": "If $\\sin^{-1}x = \\frac{1}{2}\\cos^{-1}x$, find the value of $x$.",
  "answeroption": "D",
  "explanation": "Let $\\alpha = \\sin^{-1}x$ and $\\beta = \\cos^{-1}x$.<br /><br />We know that $\\alpha + \\beta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.<br /><br />So, $\\sin^{-1}x = \\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\sin^{-1}x\\right)$.<br /><br />This gives $2\\sin^{-1}x = \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\sin^{-1}x$.<br /><br />Thus, $3\\sin^{-1}x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.<br /><br />So, $\\sin^{-1}x = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$.<br /><br />Hence, $x = \\sin\\frac{\\pi}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}$.",
